# A declaration nobody lists reaches nothing

**DONE.** `corpus()` runs a census over `decl/`, and a build fails
when the directory and the lists disagree. Three disagreements are
named separately, because they are different mistakes.

## How it was found

`decl/gc.py` was written, was well-formed, imported, parsed, and
emitted nothing at all. `nix build bindings-src` succeeded and wrote
no `gc.cpp`. The file was simply not in `NANOBIND`, and nothing said
so (`tasks/074`).

The failure looks exactly like a declaration with no classes in it.

## Why the lists are right

They are not the problem and should stay. `__init__.py` says why:

> Which declarations exist, and which of them owns a compiled module,
> is a fact about this set of documents. An emitter is handed the set;
> it does not own it.

A glob would decide by filename what the author should decide, and
`decl/README.md` is already in that directory. The list is the
statement; what is missing is the check that the directory agrees
with it.

## What this file got wrong

It named the gate as "every `*.py` in `decl/` appears in exactly one
of the three lists". That gate fails today, on the corpus it is meant
to protect. `decl/nixstore.py` and `decl/storefns.py` are in none of
the three ON PURPOSE: `gates/nbcheck.py` reads them and nothing else
does, comparing the emitter's answer against the hand-written
nanobind in `~/Code/nanopynix`. `README.md` already said so, two
paragraphs above where the plan was written.

So the census needed a fourth group before it could be exhaustive,
and `GATES` is it. "Read by a gate, not by the build" is a real fact
about a declaration, and it now has somewhere to live rather than
being the absence of three other facts.

That is the shape of the whole task in miniature. A file in no list
meant nothing, and the fix is to make every file mean something.

## What it checks

Three disagreements, each named on its own:

- a file in **no list** - the case that started this;
- a name **listed with no file** behind it, which is the other
  direction and fails later and says less;
- a file in **two lists**, which is two answers to one question. The
  census counts each name once, so without this a duplicate would
  make the totals agree by accident.

The glob decides what a declaration IS, by suffix. `decl/README.md`
is not one and `__pycache__` is a directory, so the exclusions need
no list of their own.

It sits in `corpus()` rather than in `Corpus`, and `corpus.py`'s own
docstring is the reason: "WHICH documents make up the set is a
different fact, and that one stays with the documents." `GATES` is
not passed to `Corpus` either - no emitter is handed one, and it
exists only so the census can be exhaustive.

## Proved by breaking it

The three branches have unit tests over a temporary directory. Those
prove the function and say nothing about the one line that reaches
it, so a fourth test stubs `census` and asserts `corpus()` called it
- deleting that line would otherwise leave every other test passing,
which is the same shape as the bug this task is about.

The call site was perturbed for real as well: an empty
`decl/extra.py`, and `nix build bindings-src` failed with

    TypeError: .../huggorm_decl/decl and the declaration lists
    disagree. in no list, so nothing reads them: extra.py. Add each
    to the group that describes it, or delete it.

That is the build that used to succeed and write nothing.

## What is left

The interesting version is not this. This checks one directory
against four lists; the general gate is "this input reached no
output", and it would have caught all three silent drops rather than
one. `tasks/081` holds it.

Opened 2026-09-02, while closing `tasks/074`. Closed the same day.
